  "type": "answer",
  "content": "Assuming the Payments VMs sit on esxi-pay-05: the hosts are not swapping. The likely problem is memory tiering on esxi-pay-05, whose active working set no longer fits in DRAM.\n\n**Why it is not swapping**\n- 1.6 TB consumed is 930 GB in DRAM plus 680 GB in the NVMe tier.\n- Highest ESXi DRAM Memory Consumed passes 100% by design on a tiered host (cluster-risk). 157% means 1.57 times DRAM is held, the rest in NVMe.\n- Ballooned, compressed and swapped are all 0 (cluster-consumption).\n\n**What is wrong on esxi-pay-05**\n- Active memory is 650 of 1,024 GB, 63.5% of DRAM. That is in the at-risk band of 50-75% (is-memory-tiering-slowing-host, step 2).\n- NVMe reads stayed at 380 then 360 MBps an hour later, at 290/280 µs, in the potential-issues band of 200-400 µs. After the August weekend they fell from 70 to 8 MBps at 110 µs. Reads that stay high are a sustained working set, not a warm-up (step 3).\n- sql-pay-03 has 256 GB reserved with tiering off, which shrinks the DRAM left for the 34 tiered VMs (step 7). Its 256 GB matches the 26% reservation that appeared Tue 23:25; that link is likely but unconfirmed.\n- Overcommit rose on Thu 23:35, so more VMs were added.\n\n**Why the VM metrics look fine**\n- pay-api-07's 0.2% memory contention and VM Performance 99 do not count NVMe fetches; memory latency is unaware of tiering (vm-memory-latency-high).\n- Its NVMe wait is 6 MBps × 256 × 0.00029 s ÷ 8 vCPU = 5.57% (cluster-performance). This is unverified (one page per fetch, one frozen vCPU), so it is shown, not scored.\n\n**Not a capacity problem**\n- Capacity Remaining is a 3-day forecast, read last Wednesday; Time Remaining is the long-term runway (capacity-forecast). Neither reads active memory against DRAM.\n\n**Do now**\n1. Read per-VM NVMe reads with `memstats -r vmtier-stats` on esxi-pay-05.\n2. vMotion the heaviest tiered readers to esxi-pay-07 or esxi-pay-08 (41-44% active).\n3. Re-read host NVMe reads and the Payments p99 an hour later.\n4. Get the Tue and Thu change records.\n\nNot settled by the knowledge base: whether a 5.6% NVMe wait alone explains 180 ms → 1.4 s. Check the application tier, the network and Guest OS counters too.",
