  "content": "**Most likely both changes together, made visible by the long weekend. Do not roll back CHG-2 now; relieve the six hosts that hold a SQL VM instead.** Placement of the 40 VMs is unknown, so this is not proven.\n\n**Timeline**\n- **After CHG-1 alone (Wednesday):** reservation rose to 26%, about one 256 GB SQL VM plus overhead. Active memory stayed at 46% and NVMe latency at 95 µs.\n- **After CHG-2 (Friday):** 40 × 32 GB matches the memory overcommit step from 1.50 to 1.66. Active rose to 52.7% and NVMe latency to 120 µs, a mild change.\n- **Today:** active is 63.5%, NVMe reads hold at 380 then 360 MBps, and latency is 290 µs. After the August weekend the same host fell to 8 MBps within an hour. Reads that stay high mean the working set no longer fits DRAM (is-memory-tiering-slowing-host, step 3).\n\n**Mechanism**\n- **CHG-1:** each full reservation with tiering off shrinks the DRAM left for tiered VMs (step 7).\n- **CHG-2:** it added working set on top.\n- **esxi-pay-05 (my arithmetic):** about 540 GB of tiered active memory against about 768 GB of DRAM left.\n- **Hosts without SQL:** esxi-pay-07 and esxi-pay-08 stay at 41-44% active.\n\n**Why CHG-2 looked safe:** \"Capacity Remaining (%)\" invites \"memory free now\". It is the 3-Day Forecast Capacity Remaining (%), the tightest point of a 3-day projection (capacity-forecast). It never reads active memory against DRAM, the running-stage signal for tiering (overcommit). Cluster Risk's active-memory row had already passed 50% on Friday.\n\n**Do now**\n1. Read active memory against DRAM and NVMe reads on the other five SQL hosts.\n2. Run `memstats -r vmtier-stats` on esxi-pay-05, then vMotion its heaviest NVMe readers to esxi-pay-07 and esxi-pay-08.\n3. Keep CHG-1. A full reservation with tiering off is the documented setting for latency-sensitive VMs.\n\nNot settled by the knowledge base: whether a 40-VM reverse cross-vCenter vMotion is safe mid-incident, and whether NVMe waits alone explain the jump in p99.",
